## [RULE:UOW-INTEGRITY] Transactional Integrity: Unit of Work Pattern
<!-- SECTION:transactional_integrity -->

**Critical rules — flag any violation HIGH severity:**

### Rule 1 — Services MUST call `self.uow.commit()` explicitly inside `with self.uow:`

The absence of an explicit `commit()` is the bug, not the presence of one. A missing `commit()` means silent data loss — nothing is written and no exception is raised.

```python
# CORRECT
def create_entity(self, data: EntityCreate) -> Entity:
    with self.uow:
        entity = self.uow.entities.add(Entity(**data.model_dump()))
        self.uow.commit()   # ← required; without this, nothing is persisted
    return entity
```

### Rule 2 — Repositories must NEVER call `db.commit()` or `session.commit()`

Repositories call `flush()` only (when an ID is needed immediately). A repository that commits bypasses the Unit of Work and can cause double-commits or premature flushes.

### Rule 3 — `[CALLER-UOW-REQUIRED]` methods have no own UoW block by design

Some service methods are annotated `[CALLER-UOW-REQUIRED]`. They deliberately have no `with self.uow:` block because they are designed to be called inside a caller-owned transaction.

### Rule 4 — Event publishing goes OUTSIDE `with self.uow:`, after commit

Events published inside the block will fire even if the transaction later rolls back.

```python
# CORRECT
with self.uow:
    ...
    self.uow.commit()
self.bus.publish(EntityCreatedEvent(...))   # ← after the block closes
```

